---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import CardGrid from "@components/CardGrid.astro";
import { Card, Alert, Badge } from "webcoreui/astro";
import CardAnalysis from "@components/CardAnalysis.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Maindeck Analysis - Replenish Guide"
    image="/src/assets/banners/attunement.jpeg"
    description="Deep dive into the core cards and strategy of the Replenish deck. Understand the purpose of every enchantment, cantrip, and interaction spell in the maindeck and sideboard."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="Maindeck Analysis"
        description="The post-ban Replenish maindeck is a tuned combo-control deck where every one of the 60 slots earns its place—balancing early speed, a stable manabase, and defense against fast metagames. Here's a breakdown of the staples every list runs and the flex slots that separate them."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/attunement.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Attunement"
        imageCaption="Attunement by Randy Gallegos"
        isCard={true}
    />
    <Card title="The Core" titleTag="h2">
        <CardGrid
            width="180px"
            cards={[
                {
                    src: "/src/assets/cards/sorceries/replenish.jpg",
                    alt: "Replenish",
                },
                {
                    src: "/src/assets/cards/enchantments/opalescence.jpg",
                    alt: "Opalescence",
                },
                {
                    src: "/src/assets/cards/enchantments/parallax_wave.webp",
                    alt: "Parallax Wave",
                },
                {
                    src: "/src/assets/cards/enchantments/attunement.jpg",
                    alt: "Attunement",
                },
            ]}
        />

        <div class="core-cards-list">
            <div>
                <div class="core-card-header">
                    <h3 class="core-card-title">Replenish</h3>
                    <Badge theme="secondary" small={true}
                        >4 Main · 100% Inclusion</Badge
                    >
                </div>
                <p class="m-0">
                    The cornerstone of the deck, no exceptions. It swings the
                    game with a huge, one-sided tempo hit, so you run four copies
                    no matter what the rest of your list looks like.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div>
                <div class="core-card-header">
                    <h3 class="core-card-title">Opalescence</h3>
                    <Badge theme="secondary" small={true}
                        >4 Main · 100% Inclusion</Badge
                    >
                </div>
                <p class="m-0">
                    Turns all your non-Aura enchantments into creatures with
                    power and toughness equal to their mana value. It doesn't
                    animate itself, though—you need a second copy on the
                    battlefield to turn the first into a 4/4. With one
                    Opalescence out, your enchantments become real clocks:
                    Decree of Silence (8/8), Parallax Wave (4/4), Attunement
                    (3/3), and Seal of Cleansing (2/2).
                </p>
            </div>
            <div>
                <div class="core-card-header">
                    <h3 class="core-card-title">Parallax Wave</h3>
                    <Badge theme="secondary" small={true}
                        >4 Main · 100% Inclusion</Badge
                    >
                </div>
                <p class="m-0">
                    Pulls double duty: early defense and a lethal end-game
                    engine. On its own it keeps enemy creatures in check; with
                    Opalescence out, it becomes a 4/4 that loops to permanently
                    exile their whole board, one card at a time.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div>
                <div class="core-card-header">
                    <h3 class="core-card-title">Attunement</h3>
                    <Badge theme="secondary" small={true}
                        >4 Main · 100% Inclusion</Badge
                    >
                </div>
                <p class="m-0">
                    Your main selection engine: it digs three cards deep, stocks
                    the graveyard for Replenish, and swings as a 3/3 under
                    Opalescence. Don't fire it blindly. Before you activate, know
                    exactly which cards you're discarding. If your hand already
                    has a plan and nothing spare to pitch, leave it alone and
                    hold the activation.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </Card>

    <Card title="The Lock Piece" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Decree of Silence"
            badge="2–3 Main · 98.3% Inclusion"
            image="enchantments/decree_of_silence.jpg"
        >
            <p class="mt-0">
                With Parallax Tide gone, Decree is the deck's best lock piece,
                and it's good in more ways than one. Landing the full
                Opal-Wave-Decree combo just wins on the spot, but even the
                simple line—pitch Decree to Attunement or Frantic Search, bring
                it back with Replenish—forces your opponent to claw through
                three countered spells to do anything.
            </p>
            <p>
                Against control, cycling Decree for six mana is an
                uncounterable cantrip that strips a key counter or threat while
                keeping your cards flowing. Opponents often hand it to you off an
                Intuition pile, figuring an eight-mana enchantment is dead—then
                eat the cycling trigger and lose a ton of tempo. And under
                Opalescence, it comes down as an 8/8 finisher.
            </p>
            <p>
                For when to run two copies versus three depending on the
                metagame, check the comparison on the <a
                    href="/deck"
                    class="guide-link">Lists page</a
                >.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Proactive Disruption" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Meddling Mage"
            badge="0–4 Main · 10.6% MD / 92.2% SB"
            image="creatures/meddling_mage.webp"
        >
            <p class="mt-0">
                Most lists save their creatures for after sideboard, so the
                opponent's Game 1 removal rots in their hand. Maindecking
                Meddling Mage instead gives you an early, disruptive shield
                against fast combo and counter-heavy control right away.
            </p>
            <p class="mb-0">
                Getting the most out of Meddling Mage means reading the
                metagame and knowing which spell each deck can't function
                without. For what to name in each matchup and how the
                creature-transformation plan works, see our <a
                    href="/sideboard"
                    class="guide-link">Sideboard Analysis</a
                > and <a href="/matchups" class="guide-link">Matchup Matrix</a>.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Abeyance"
            badge="2–3 Main · 72.8% MD / 42.2% SB"
            image="instants/abeyance.jpg"
        >
            <p class="mt-0">
                A staple in competitive lists. Abeyance protects your combo
                turn and doubles as a tempo play against the opponent. It stops
                them from casting instants and sorceries or activating
                permanents for the rest of the turn, so casting it right before <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > means your reanimation resolves untouched—and shuts off artifacts
                like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                > in the process.
            </p>
            <p class="mb-0">
                And since Abeyance draws you a card when it resolves, it never
                really costs you anything—cast it on the opponent's upkeep to
                stall their turn, turn off a <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Wasteland">Wasteland</a
                > activation, or shut down a creature with an activated ability.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Card Selection" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Frantic Search"
            badge="3–4 Main · 99.4% Inclusion"
            image="instants/frantic_search.webp"
        >
            <p class="m-0">
                Keeps your deck moving and sets up explosive turns alongside sol
                lands or painlands. But it costs you a card, so it's the first
                thing you trim after sideboard against grindy control decks,
                where every card counts.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Intuition"
            badge="3–4 Main · 99.4% Inclusion"
            image="instants/intuition.webp"
        >
            <p class="m-0">
                The deck's most flexible selection engine, and the one that
                tests your skill the most. The default pile is Opalescence,
                Parallax Wave, and Decree of Silence—whichever one the opponent
                gives you, the other two go to the yard to fuel a Replenish. But
                the pile has to fit the board: three lands against Landstill,
                three Swords to Plowshares to survive fast aggro. Against
                reactive blue decks, people often cut a copy to keep their
                threat count up. For a full walkthrough on building piles and
                reading tricky spots, see our <a
                    href="/intuition"
                    class="guide-link">Intuition Cheatsheet</a
                >.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Why Not Careful Study?"
            badge="Omitted from Maindeck"
        >
            <p class="m-0">
                While <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Careful Study">Careful Study</a
                > looks tempting as a one-mana way to sculpt the graveyard, but it
                doesn't make the cut. <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                > keeps coming back as an enchantment engine, and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > untaps your sol lands for free velocity—Careful Study just costs
                you a card the moment it resolves. Bleeding cards to dump enchantments
                burns through your counters and removal before you ever reach the
                four-mana Replenish that wins the game.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Interaction & Permission" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Swords to Plowshares"
            badge="3–4 Main · 99.4% Inclusion"
            image="instants/swords_to_plowshares.webp"
        >
            <p class="m-0">
                The gold standard for one-mana removal. Three in the maindeck is
                the clean number; bring in the fourth after board against
                creature-heavy decks.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Seal of Cleansing"
            badge="2–3 Main · 97.8% Inclusion"
            image="enchantments/seal_of_cleansing.webp"
        >
            <p class="m-0">
                A proactive answer to problem enchantments and mana artifacts.
                You can crack it for value at instant speed and bring it back
                with Replenish, so it keeps generating answers all game. Easy to
                cut against decks it can't hit.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Mana Leak"
            badge="2–3 Main · 35.6% Inclusion"
            image="instants/mana_leak.webp"
        >
            <p class="m-0">
                Maindecking Mana Leak is a proven call—over 35% of pilots ran
                it after the ban. In fast fields full of combo or reactive
                mirrors like Esper Psychatog and tempo Replenish, cheap two-mana
                countermagic buys you the early turns you need. It gets worse in
                long games against <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Standstill">Landstill</a
                >, once they can just pay the two, but holding up two mana still
                forces them into awkward sequencing while you set up.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Proactive Disruption & Mana Denial" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Tsabo’s Web"
            badge="1–3 Main · 24.4% MD / 77.8% SB"
            image="artifacts/tsabos_web.jpg"
        >
            <p class="m-0">
                A premier proactive answer to opponent utility lands like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Rishadan Port">Rishadan Port</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Dust Bowl"
                    >Dust Bowl</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Wasteland"
                    >Wasteland</a
                >, and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Mishra's Factory">Mishra's Factory</a
                >. Think of it less as removal and more as mana denial: cast it
                early and their taxing lands and creature-lands stop mattering
                before they can slow your setup or race your combo—and it
                replaces itself with a card, so you lose nothing.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Grinding & Card Advantage Engines" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Deep Analysis"
            badge="0–1 Flex · 3.3% Inclusion"
            image="sorceries/deep_analysis.webp"
        >
            <p class="mt-0">
                A strong utility card that turns an early <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Intuition">Intuition</a
                > into a lasting draw engine in slower matchups. Against counter-heavy
                control, tutoring straight for combo pieces leaves your threats exposed
                to graveyard hate; grabbing Deep Analysis instead keeps you drawing from
                both hand and graveyard. One copy in the maindeck is a fine way to
                out-grind draw-go decks.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Fact or Fiction"
            badge="0–2 Flex · 2.8% Inclusion"
            image="instants/fact_or_fiction.webp"
        >
            <p class="mt-0">
                A solid pick for reactive builds that want to hold up counters
                like Mana Leak and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Counterspell">Counterspell</a
                >. Four mana is a real cost, but digging five deep at the end of
                their turn lets you dump enchantments into the graveyard or find
                defensive answers without giving up tempo.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="The Mana Base" titleTag="h2">
        <h3 class="mt-0">Mana Base Theory</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>25 vs. 26 Lands:</strong> Tournament lists split about evenly
            between 25 and 26 lands, and both are fine. On 25 lands, players usually
            run only three Skycloud Expanse to keep as many basics as possible against
            Wasteland and Price of Progress. On 26, that extra slot almost always goes
            to the fourth Skycloud Expanse, for better color filtering and smoother
            mana into your four-drop turns.
        </p>
        <div>
            <div class="core-card-header">
                <h3 class="core-card-title">Ten Basics</h3>
                <Badge theme="secondary" small={true}
                    >10 Main (5 Island · 5 Plains)</Badge
                >
            </div>
            <p class="mt-0">
                Five basic Islands and five basic Plains keep you safe from
                non-basic punishment like Wasteland and Price of Progress.
                Getting your first three land drops as basics matters a lot
                against burn and mana-denial decks. It also leaves you with
                about 16 sources of each color, enough to cast your
                double-pipped four-drops on time.
            </p>
        </div>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Ancient Tomb"
            badge="4 Main · 100% Inclusion"
            image="lands/ancient_tomb.jpg"
        >
            <p class="mt-0">
                Your key "sol land," making two colorless on turn one—it's the
                deck's main accelerant. It powers out a turn-two <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Intuition">Intuition</a
                > or <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                >, and lets you land turn-three combo pieces like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence"
                    >Opalescence</a
                > ahead of curve, all without skipping a land drop.
            </p>
            <p class="mb-0">
                Untapping Ancient Tomb with Frantic Search even nets you an
                extra colorless mana, which fuels big turns when you're
                otherwise starved for mana. Because it changes the math on your
                early plays, Ancient Tomb often turns a shaky opener into an easy
                keep; our <a
                    href="/mulligan#the-ancient-tomb-acceleration-factor"
                    class="guide-link">Mulligan Guide</a
                > and <a href="/mechanics" class="guide-link"
                    >Mechanics Masterclass</a
                > go deeper on the details.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Skycloud Expanse"
            badge="3–4 Main · 99.4% Inclusion"
            image="lands/skycloud_expanse.webp"
        >
            <p class="mt-0">
                Your main fixer—it turns colorless mana from sol lands and
                basics into blue or white. Three copies in 25-land builds keeps
                you resilient against non-basic hate; four in 26-land builds
                gives you rock-solid color for your busy turns.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Why Not City of Traitors?"
            badge="Omitted from Maindeck"
        >
            <p class="m-0">
                Without <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Tide">Parallax Tide</a
                > to close games out fast, post-ban Replenish leans on grinding
                into the mid and late game.
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="City of Traitors"
                    >City of Traitors</a
                > makes your sequencing painful and quietly costs you board presence
                in long attrition games.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <div style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem; margin-top: 1rem;">
            <Alert theme="warning">
                <strong>Turn-One Sequencing</strong><br />As a general rule,
                lead with a dual land to keep your color options open for
                whatever you draw turn two. But if you think your opponent has
                Wasteland, lead with a basic instead so you don't get blown out
                on tempo.
            </Alert>
        </div>
    </Card>

    <Card>
        <p
            style="margin-bottom: 0; text-align: center; color: var(--w-color-primary-30);"
        >
            Now that you know the 60, head to the full Sideboard Analysis to
            round out your 75.
        </p>
    </Card>
</Layout>

<style>
    .core-cards-list {
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: 1.5rem;
        margin-top: 2rem;
    }

    .core-card-header {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.75rem;
        margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
    }

    .core-card-title {
        margin: 0;
        color: var(--w-color-primary-10);
    }
</style>
